+Of the $136$ losing runs whose start is known, $131$ are (i), $1$ is (ii) and $4$ are (iii), and all five that are not (i)
+are linear probe then fine-tune on $ee\to Z\gamma$ (rungs $1$, $7$, $9$ at $D=10$ and $10^{1.5}$). The one (ii) is rung $7$
+at $D=10^{1.5}$ at the default's HPs, the run par.~\ref{par:lowd_ft} already noted: it starts at $8.1\times10^{-3}$,
+scratch's best is $1.0\times10^{-2}$, and its best is $2.4\times10^{-2}$; with its own search the same cell does not
+lose. A run that keeps the parent's head starts far from anything scratch reaches: the parent's output on the probe as
+it is leaves a median $0.50$ of the target's variance unexplained and sits $1.7$ decades above the read-out of the
+same trunk. The read-out beats scratch in $25$ of the $60$ cells ($ee\to d\bar d$ $11$ of its $12$, $ee\to b\bar b$ at
+one loop $10$, $ud\to ud$ $3$, $ee\to Z\gamma$ $1$, $u\bar u\to gg$ $0$), but not where the runs lose: over the losing
+runs of each round and method its median is $7.2$ to $21\times$ scratch's best, and only $4$ losing runs had a
+read-out below scratch (three L2-SP runs and the grid's rung-$5$ default, on $ee\to Z\gamma$ at $10^{1.5}$ and $ud\to
+ud$ at $10^2$). The fine-tune does throw away part of a start in its first updates, and mostly recovers: the first
+validation of linear probe then fine-tune is above its read-out start in $50$ and $46$ of the $60$ runs (first round,
+DyHPO round), at $D=10$, where it follows a single update, by a median $3.8$ and $1.5\times$, and the run's best ends
+below that start in $53$ and $57$. The runs that keep the parent's head end above what a read-out of their frozen trunk
+gives in $59$ of $300$ (the default $11$ of $60$, L2-SP $27$ of $120$, the frozen blocks $21$ of $120$), linear probe
+then fine-tune in $10$ of $120$. So at these $D$ fine-tuning does not undo a start that beats scratch, but in one run:
+the losing runs start worse than scratch's best, with the best linear head as well, improve, and stop short of it. What
+costs is the starting point itself: from the same $D$ events, steps and five-trial search, scratch's random
+initialization ends below the pretrained one in these cells, so there the pretrained weights are a worse place to start
+than random ones (the pattern of rung $6$'s basin on $u\bar u\to Zg$, par.~\ref{par:nogain}), not a good start the
+fine-tune damages. Whether that is a basin the run cannot leave or weights that are simply worse than random is open.
